1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,244
Normandy, nineteen forty-four.

2
00:00:02,244 --> 00:00:05,567
This American soldier is
about to cross one field.

3
00:00:05,567 --> 00:00:08,890
He carries a rifle, a
shovel, and one day of food.

4
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:10,770
But he is not alone.

5
00:00:10,770 --> 00:00:14,227
Behind him stands a line of
people no war movie shows.

6
00:00:14,227 --> 00:00:15,635
How long is that line?

7
00:00:15,635 --> 00:00:18,260
By the end, you'll know the exact number.

8
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:21,022
Let's start with something simple.

9
00:00:21,022 --> 00:00:21,658
Breakfast.

10
00:00:21,658 --> 00:00:24,711
At the front, our soldier
often eats a K ration.

11
00:00:24,711 --> 00:00:27,000
A small waxed cardboard box of food.

12
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:29,116
Three boxes make one day.

13
00:00:29,116 --> 00:00:30,934
Breakfast, dinner, and supper.

14
00:00:30,934 --> 00:00:35,360
Together, they hold about two thousand
eight hundred and thirty calories.

15
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:38,488
Each box was planned
down to small details.

16
00:00:38,488 --> 00:00:43,014
Besides the food, there were a few
cigarettes, chewing gum, and toilet paper.

17
00:00:43,014 --> 00:00:46,130
Somebody, in an office far
away, decided all of that.

18
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:48,312
That sounds like plenty.

19
00:00:48,312 --> 00:00:49,169
But it isn't.

20
00:00:49,169 --> 00:00:53,585
A man who digs holes and carries
ammunition all day burns far more.

21
00:00:53,585 --> 00:00:57,210
So front-line soldiers were
hungry almost all the time.

22
00:00:57,810 --> 00:00:59,567
Now ask a strange question.

23
00:00:59,567 --> 00:01:00,739
Who made this box?

24
00:01:00,739 --> 00:01:01,845
Not an army cook.

25
00:01:01,845 --> 00:01:05,880
A factory, thousands of miles
away, back in the United States.

26
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:07,926
Farmers grew the food.

27
00:01:07,926 --> 00:01:09,635
Factory workers packed it.

28
00:01:09,635 --> 00:01:11,935
Railroad crews hauled it to a port.

29
00:01:11,935 --> 00:01:14,170
Dockworkers lifted it onto a ship.

30
00:01:14,770 --> 00:01:17,008
So before our soldier takes one bite,

31
00:01:17,008 --> 00:01:20,153
dozens of people have already
touched his breakfast.

32
00:01:20,153 --> 00:01:21,708
But food is the easy part.

33
00:01:21,708 --> 00:01:23,024
Food does not explode.

34
00:01:23,024 --> 00:01:24,400
And food does not sink.

35
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,767
Everything this soldier uses has
to cross the Atlantic Ocean.

36
00:01:28,767 --> 00:01:32,040
That is more than three
thousand miles of open water.

37
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:36,916
But German submarines, the U-boats,
were hunting the ships that carried it.

38
00:01:36,916 --> 00:01:41,020
Every ship that went down took bullets,
food, and sometimes men with it.

39
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:43,223
So America needed ships.

40
00:01:43,223 --> 00:01:43,891
Not a few.

41
00:01:43,891 --> 00:01:44,559
Thousands.

42
00:01:44,559 --> 00:01:46,697
The answer was the Liberty ship.

43
00:01:46,697 --> 00:01:51,440
A simple, slow cargo ship, built
almost like a car on an assembly line.

44
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:56,650
Many of the welders in those shipyards
had never held a torch before the war.

45
00:01:56,650 --> 00:01:58,207
Plenty of them were women.

46
00:01:58,207 --> 00:02:01,320
They learned in weeks a job
that used to take years.

47
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:06,090
American shipyards built two thousand
seven hundred and ten of them.

48
00:02:06,090 --> 00:02:08,137
One of them, the Robert E. Peary,

49
00:02:08,137 --> 00:02:11,610
went from keel to launch in
four days and fifteen hours.

50
00:02:12,210 --> 00:02:13,745
But a ship needs a crew.

51
00:02:13,745 --> 00:02:18,606
The men who sailed these cargo ships
were mostly civilian merchant mariners.

52
00:02:18,606 --> 00:02:19,438
Not soldiers.

53
00:02:19,438 --> 00:02:22,380
Yet their job was one of
the deadliest at sea.

54
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:24,612
A ship, in less than a week.

55
00:02:24,612 --> 00:02:26,886
That sounds like the problem is solved.

56
00:02:26,886 --> 00:02:29,101
But a ship only gets you to the coast.

57
00:02:29,101 --> 00:02:32,890
And by the end of summer, our
soldier was nowhere near the coast.

58
00:02:33,490 --> 00:02:38,052
By late August nineteen forty-four, the
Allied armies had raced across France.

59
00:02:38,052 --> 00:02:40,918
The front was hundreds of
miles from the beaches.

60
00:02:40,918 --> 00:02:43,960
And the French railways had
been wrecked by bombing.

61
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:46,567
So the Army did something simple.

62
00:02:46,567 --> 00:02:47,480
It used trucks.

63
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:52,590
Thousands of trucks, driving day and night
on one-way roads closed to everyone else.

64
00:02:52,590 --> 00:02:54,780
They called it the Red Ball Express.

65
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:58,039
Most of those trucks were
the two-and-a-half-ton

66
00:02:58,039 --> 00:03:00,864
workhorse the soldiers
called the deuce and a half.

67
00:03:00,864 --> 00:03:03,770
Each one was loaded far
beyond what it was built for.

68
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:08,949
On August twenty-ninth, nineteen
forty-four, the Red Ball set its record.

69
00:03:08,949 --> 00:03:11,613
Five thousand nine hundred and fifty-eight

70
00:03:11,613 --> 00:03:14,846
vehicles carried over twelve
thousand tons forward.

71
00:03:14,846 --> 00:03:15,850
In a single day.

72
00:03:16,450 --> 00:03:20,137
About three out of four of those
drivers were Black soldiers.

73
00:03:20,137 --> 00:03:22,131
They served in a segregated army.

74
00:03:22,131 --> 00:03:24,730
They drove for days
with very little sleep.

75
00:03:25,330 --> 00:03:27,522
Some drivers fell asleep at the wheel.

76
00:03:27,522 --> 00:03:29,540
Trucks broke down by the thousands.

77
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:31,232
But the convoys kept rolling,

78
00:03:31,232 --> 00:03:34,500
because the front was screaming
for one thing above all.

79
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:36,571
There was a cruel twist.

80
00:03:36,571 --> 00:03:40,985
The longer the road got, the more
gasoline the trucks themselves burned.

81
00:03:40,985 --> 00:03:44,480
A big share of the fuel was
used up just delivering fuel.

82
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:45,665
Gasoline.

83
00:03:45,665 --> 00:03:47,551
And even this was not enough.

84
00:03:47,551 --> 00:03:52,948
At the end of August, General Patton's tanks
ground to a halt near the Meuse river.

85
00:03:52,948 --> 00:03:54,704
Not because of the Germans.

86
00:03:54,704 --> 00:03:56,330
Because the fuel ran out.

87
00:03:56,930 --> 00:03:58,891
So here is our first big clue.

88
00:03:58,891 --> 00:04:01,375
Fighting a war is not mostly fighting.

89
00:04:01,375 --> 00:04:02,683
It is moving things.

90
00:04:02,683 --> 00:04:04,709
And moving things takes people.

91
00:04:04,709 --> 00:04:05,690
Lots of people.

92
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:08,450
But soldiers do not run on gasoline.

93
00:04:08,450 --> 00:04:09,470
They run on hope.

94
00:04:09,470 --> 00:04:12,710
And at the front, hope usually
arrived in an envelope.

95
00:04:13,310 --> 00:04:15,528
Millions of letters crossed the ocean.

96
00:04:15,528 --> 00:04:16,637
But paper is heavy.

97
00:04:16,637 --> 00:04:21,190
Every sack of mail on a ship was space
that could have carried shells or food.

98
00:04:21,790 --> 00:04:23,271
So the Army got clever.

99
00:04:23,271 --> 00:04:26,489
Letters were photographed
onto tiny rolls of film.

100
00:04:26,489 --> 00:04:29,000
They called it Victory Mail, or V mail.

101
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:34,479
Thirty-seven mail bags of ordinary letters
shrank into one single bag of film.

102
00:04:34,479 --> 00:04:39,670
On the other side of the ocean, the film
was printed back onto paper and delivered.

103
00:04:40,270 --> 00:04:42,769
For a nineteen-year-old in a muddy hole,

104
00:04:42,769 --> 00:04:47,142
that letter could be the only proof
the world back home still existed.

105
00:04:47,142 --> 00:04:49,123
Mothers wrote about the weather.

106
00:04:49,123 --> 00:04:50,918
Sisters wrote about weddings.

107
00:04:50,918 --> 00:04:52,590
Nobody wrote about the war.

108
00:04:53,190 --> 00:04:56,990
Which means that somewhere behind
the front, soldiers spent their

109
00:04:56,990 --> 00:05:00,789
whole war printing, sorting, and
delivering letters to strangers.

110
00:05:00,789 --> 00:05:02,588
Nobody makes movies about them.

111
00:05:02,588 --> 00:05:05,430
But every man at the front
waited for their work.

112
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:08,141
Now the hardest moment of the day.

113
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:09,569
Our soldier is wounded.

114
00:05:09,569 --> 00:05:11,432
Not badly, but he cannot walk.

115
00:05:11,432 --> 00:05:12,550
What happens next?

116
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:15,020
First, a medic reaches him.

117
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:16,337
He has no hospital.

118
00:05:16,337 --> 00:05:19,108
Just a canvas bag and his own two hands.

119
00:05:19,108 --> 00:05:21,117
His job is simple and brutal.

120
00:05:21,117 --> 00:05:21,740
Buy time.

121
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:24,266
Litter bearers carry him back.

122
00:05:24,266 --> 00:05:26,512
A battalion aid station checks him.

123
00:05:26,512 --> 00:05:31,070
An ambulance drives him to a field
hospital, where surgeons go to work.

124
00:05:31,670 --> 00:05:33,645
Speed mattered more than anything.

125
00:05:33,645 --> 00:05:38,176
A wounded man who reached a surgeon
quickly had a far better chance of living.

126
00:05:38,176 --> 00:05:40,790
So the whole chain was
built to save minutes.

127
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:43,465
Some wounded men went even further.

128
00:05:43,465 --> 00:05:44,413
Back to England.

129
00:05:44,413 --> 00:05:45,717
Some all the way home.

130
00:05:45,717 --> 00:05:49,630
Every step of that trip is handled
by a different group of people.

131
00:05:50,230 --> 00:05:52,266
So let's count who we have met.

132
00:05:52,266 --> 00:05:52,792
Farmers.

133
00:05:52,792 --> 00:05:53,843
Factory workers.

134
00:05:53,843 --> 00:05:54,697
Shipbuilders.

135
00:05:54,697 --> 00:05:55,222
Sailors.

136
00:05:55,222 --> 00:05:56,142
Truck drivers.

137
00:05:56,142 --> 00:05:56,930
Mail clerks.

138
00:05:56,930 --> 00:05:57,390
Medics.

139
00:05:57,390 --> 00:05:57,981
Surgeons.

140
00:05:57,981 --> 00:06:00,740
How many of them stand behind one soldier?

141
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:04,051
To answer that, the Army
used a strange word.

142
00:06:04,051 --> 00:06:04,533
A slice.

143
00:06:04,533 --> 00:06:05,316
Not of bread.

144
00:06:05,316 --> 00:06:06,340
A division slice.

145
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:08,880
A division is a big army unit.

146
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:12,341
In nineteen forty-four, an
American infantry division

147
00:06:12,341 --> 00:06:15,540
had a little over fourteen
thousand men on paper.

148
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:18,128
But planners noticed something.

149
00:06:18,128 --> 00:06:20,919
Every division in the
line needed many more

150
00:06:20,919 --> 00:06:23,645
men behind it, in the same theater of war.

151
00:06:23,645 --> 00:06:25,120
Just to keep it moving.

152
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:30,554
The division, plus its share of everyone
supporting it, is the division slice.

153
00:06:30,554 --> 00:06:33,963
And the slice was much bigger
than the division itself.

154
00:06:33,963 --> 00:06:34,954
How much bigger?

155
00:06:34,954 --> 00:06:36,070
Hold that thought.

156
00:06:36,670 --> 00:06:39,972
Because first, the Army hit
a problem nobody expected.

157
00:06:39,972 --> 00:06:44,437
By late nineteen forty-four, America
had the biggest army in its history.

158
00:06:44,437 --> 00:06:47,250
Around eight million
people in the Army alone.

159
00:06:47,850 --> 00:06:51,889
But in Europe, generals were
running out of one kind of soldier.

160
00:06:51,889 --> 00:06:52,647
Not drivers.

161
00:06:52,647 --> 00:06:53,278
Not cooks.

162
00:06:53,278 --> 00:06:53,972
Not clerks.

163
00:06:53,972 --> 00:06:54,540
Riflemen.

164
00:06:55,140 --> 00:06:59,112
Rifle companies were losing men
faster than new ones could arrive.

165
00:06:59,112 --> 00:07:02,332
Over months of fighting,
some infantry divisions lost

166
00:07:02,332 --> 00:07:05,310
more men in total than
they had on the first day.

167
00:07:05,910 --> 00:07:08,316
So the Army pulled men from other jobs.

168
00:07:08,316 --> 00:07:11,618
Anti-aircraft gunners, clerks,
and air force trainees

169
00:07:11,618 --> 00:07:14,670
were sent to infantry
training and handed rifles.

170
00:07:15,270 --> 00:07:18,313
In late December nineteen
forty-four, the Army even

171
00:07:18,313 --> 00:07:21,355
asked Black service troops
to volunteer for combat.

172
00:07:21,355 --> 00:07:22,892
Thousands stepped forward.

173
00:07:22,892 --> 00:07:26,850
Many sergeants gave up their stripes,
just for the chance to fight.

174
00:07:27,450 --> 00:07:32,575
About two thousand two hundred of them went
to the front in thirty-seven platoons.

175
00:07:32,575 --> 00:07:34,388
So here is the real question.

176
00:07:34,388 --> 00:07:37,700
How can an army of eight
million run out of riflemen?

177
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:39,538
Let's do the math.

178
00:07:39,538 --> 00:07:41,877
Our soldier is in a rifle company.

179
00:07:41,877 --> 00:07:45,936
A rifle company had about one
hundred and ninety-three men.

180
00:07:45,936 --> 00:07:49,100
An infantry division had
twenty-seven of them.

181
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:52,853
Twenty-seven times one
hundred and ninety-three

182
00:07:52,853 --> 00:07:55,469
is about five thousand two hundred men.

183
00:07:55,469 --> 00:07:57,126
Out of fourteen thousand.

184
00:07:57,126 --> 00:08:01,370
So even inside a division, most
men were not in rifle companies.

185
00:08:01,970 --> 00:08:03,435
Now zoom out to Europe.

186
00:08:03,435 --> 00:08:07,417
In June nineteen forty-five, each
American division there came

187
00:08:07,417 --> 00:08:11,270
with a slice of about thirty-five
thousand five hundred men.

188
00:08:11,870 --> 00:08:14,967
Divide the slice by the
men in rifle companies.

189
00:08:14,967 --> 00:08:16,747
The answer is almost seven.

190
00:08:16,747 --> 00:08:21,360
Almost seven Americans in Europe for
every one man in a rifle company.

191
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:24,969
Six other men in the
theater for every rifleman.

192
00:08:24,969 --> 00:08:28,855
Drivers, engineers, gunners,
mechanics, cooks, clerks, medics.

193
00:08:28,855 --> 00:08:31,362
All of them needed, all of them working.

194
00:08:31,362 --> 00:08:34,120
But only one of them
walks across the field.

195
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:35,936
But we are not done.

196
00:08:35,936 --> 00:08:40,377
Add the air force, the training camps,
and everyone in uniform back home.

197
00:08:40,377 --> 00:08:43,479
Divide the whole Army by
its eighty-nine divisions.

198
00:08:43,479 --> 00:08:45,790
That is over ninety thousand men each.

199
00:08:46,390 --> 00:08:49,128
Divide that by the men in rifle companies.

200
00:08:49,128 --> 00:08:50,106
About eighteen.

201
00:08:50,106 --> 00:08:52,941
For every soldier walking
toward the enemy,

202
00:08:52,941 --> 00:08:55,908
seventeen others in
uniform stood behind him.

203
00:08:55,908 --> 00:08:59,950
And that is before we count a
single farmer or factory worker.

204
00:09:00,550 --> 00:09:01,824
This was not waste.

205
00:09:01,824 --> 00:09:07,390
It is why the American soldier usually had
food, ammunition, and letters from home.

206
00:09:07,390 --> 00:09:09,200
But the system had a price.

207
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,890
Infantrymen were only a
small part of the Army.

208
00:09:12,890 --> 00:09:15,618
Yet of every four Army soldiers killed in

209
00:09:15,618 --> 00:09:18,545
ground combat, about
three were infantrymen.

210
00:09:18,545 --> 00:09:22,030
A tiny tip on a giant spear,
taking almost every hit.

211
00:09:22,630 --> 00:09:27,301
So the next time you watch a war movie,
remember the line behind the soldier.

212
00:09:27,301 --> 00:09:30,880
Seventeen people working so one
man could cross that field.

213
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:33,670
And one man, carrying
the war for all of them.

214
00:09:34,270 --> 00:09:36,896
Germany built its army very differently.

215
00:09:36,896 --> 00:09:41,163
It invaded the Soviet Union with
hundreds of thousands of horses.

216
00:09:41,163 --> 00:09:41,425
Why?

217
00:09:41,425 --> 00:09:42,935
That is the next video.

218
00:09:42,935 --> 00:09:44,970
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
